("PRN-031","they – them – their – theirs – themselves","The reflexive is 'themselves'; 'theirselves' is non-standard.","THEM + SELVES.","'By themselves' = alone, without help.","Write a sentence about something children can do by themselves.",None,"themselves vs each other","They hurt themselves = each one hurt himself/herself. They hurt each other = A hurt B and B hurt A."),
("PRN-032","this – these (plural)","With a plural noun, use 'these', not 'this'.","THIS = one, near. THESE = many, near.","'This one' is used when pointing: 'I'll take this one.'","Write a sentence using 'this' and one using 'these'.",None,"this vs these","This + singular (this shoe). These + plural (these shoes)."),
("PRN-033","that – those (plural)","'Information' is uncountable, so it has no plural 'informations'.","THAT = one, far. THOSE = many, far.","'That's it' = that's all.","Write a sentence using 'that' about something far away.",None,"that vs those","That + singular or uncountable (that information). Those + plural (those books)."),
("PRN-034","this – these (plural)","'These' needs a plural noun and verb: 'These books are…'.","THESE has an E-S-E: plural, many.","'These days' = nowadays.","Write a sentence using 'these days'.",None,"these vs this","These + plural (these books). This + singular (this book).","These book is mine.","These books are mine."),
("PRN-035","that – those (plural)","To talk about a past period, say 'in those days'.","THOSE has an S: plural, many, far.","'In those days' = in that past period.","Write a sentence starting 'In those days,…'.",None,"those vs these","Those = far away or in the past. These = near or now.","Those days, we lived in Delhi.","In those days, we lived in Delhi."),
("PRN-036","who – whom – whose","For the object of a verb, formal English uses 'whom'; everyday English often uses 'who'. After 'did', use the base verb 'give'.","WHO = HE (subject). WHOM = HIM (object). Both end in M: HIM/WHOM.","'Who' is fine in speech for objects: 'Who did you meet?'","Write a question starting with 'Who…?'.",None,"who vs whom","Who = subject (Who called?). Whom = object, formal (To whom did you write?)."),
("PRN-037","who – whom – whose","For the subject of a sentence, use 'who', not 'whom'.","WHOM only after verbs or prepositions: 'to whom', 'for whom'.","'To whom it may concern' is a formal letter opening.","Write a formal sentence with 'to whom'.",None,"whom vs who","Whom = object only. Who = subject (and informal object)."),
("PRN-038","who – whom – whose","'Whose' shows possession; 'who's' means 'who is' or 'who has'.","WHO'S = WHO IS. If you can't say 'who is', write WHOSE.","'Whose turn is it?' is common in games.","Write a question starting with 'Whose…?'.",None,"whose vs who's","Whose = belonging to whom. Who's = who is / who has."),
("PRN-039",P,"Use 'which' when choosing from a small, known set.","WHICH = choose from these few.","'Which one?' = which of these?","Write a question with 'Which … do you prefer?'.",None,"which vs what","Which = choose from a known set. What = open question, any answer."),
("PRN-040",P,"For open questions like a name, use 'what', not 'which'.","WHAT = anything, open.","'What?' alone can sound rude; 'Sorry?' or 'Pardon?' is politer.","Write three questions starting with 'What'.",None,"what vs which","What = open, unlimited choice. Which = limited choice."),
("PRN-041",P,"'Someone' always takes a singular verb.","SOME + ONE: one person, so singular.","'Someone' is used in positive sentences; 'anyone' in questions.","Write a sentence about someone who helped you.",None,"someone vs anyone","Someone = positive (someone called). Anyone = questions, negatives (did anyone call?)."),
("PRN-042",P,"'Anyone' always takes a singular verb.","ANY + ONE: one person, so singular.","'Anyone can do it' = it's easy.","Write a question using 'Does anyone…?'.",None,"anyone vs any one","Anyone = any person. Any one = any single item (any one of these books)."),
("PRN-043",P,"'Everyone' always takes a singular verb.","EVERY + ONE: each person, singular verb.","'Everyone' can use 'their': 'Everyone has their own opinion.'","Write a sentence starting 'Everyone in my family…'.",None,"everyone vs every one","Everyone = all people. Every one = each single item."),
("PRN-044",P,"'No one' is already negative, so don't add 'doesn't'.","NO + ONE: two words. 'Noone' is wrong.","'No one' and 'nobody' mean the same.","Write a sentence using 'no one'.",None,"no one vs none","No one = no person. None = not any (of things or people)."),
("PRN-045",P,"'Somebody' always takes a singular verb.","SOME + BODY: one person.","'Somebody' is slightly more informal than 'someone'.","Write a sentence about somebody you met today.",None,"somebody vs someone","Same meaning. Somebody is slightly more informal."),
("PRN-046",P,"'Anybody' takes a singular verb: 'Is anybody home?'","ANY + BODY: one person.","'Anybody there?' is a common call into an empty room.","Write a question using 'Is anybody…?'.",None,"anybody vs anyone","Same meaning. Anybody is slightly more informal."),
("PRN-047",P,"'Everybody' takes a singular verb: 'Everybody was happy'.","EVERY + BODY: each person.","'Everybody knows that…' introduces common knowledge.","Write a sentence about something everybody likes.",None,"everybody vs everyone","Same meaning. Everybody is slightly more informal."),
("PRN-048",P,"'Nobody' is already negative, so don't add 'doesn't'.","NO + BODY: no person.","'Nobody's perfect' = everyone makes mistakes.","Write a sentence using 'nobody'.",None,"nobody vs no one","Same meaning. Nobody is slightly more informal."),
("PRN-049",P,"'Something' always takes a singular verb.","SOME + THING: one thing.","'Something like that' = approximately that.","Write a sentence about something you want to buy.",None,"something vs anything","Something = positive (I want something). Anything = questions, negatives (I don't want anything)."),
("PRN-050",P,"In negative sentences, use 'anything', not 'something'.","ANY + THING: any thing at all.","'Anything else?' is common in shops.","Write a negative sentence using 'anything'.",None,"anything vs something","Anything = questions and negatives. Something = positives and offers."),
("PRP-001",Q,"To show the topic, use 'about', not 'for'.","ABOUT = the topic around which the talk goes.","'About' also means 'approximately': 'about ₹500'.","Write a sentence about a book and what it is about.",None,"about vs on","About = general topic (a talk about India). On = specific or academic topic (a lecture on economics)."),
("PRP-002",Q,"'Above' takes its object directly; 'from' is not needed.","ABOVE = higher than, not touching.","'Above all' = most importantly.","Write a sentence about something above your bed.",None,"above vs over","Above = higher than (not touching). Over = higher than, covering, or moving across."),
